// The Appeal, ch10: Non-contributory. Monday, June 14 to Wednesday, August 18, 2027 (the score on Wednesday, June 30; Teo's kitchen table on Saturday, July 10; the kitchen table at home that night; Sunday morning; Federal on Tuesday, July 20; the Wednesday call on July 21; Ana's call and the Wednesday call on August 18).
// Coverage per the bible: scenes LR18x4 LR17x2 LR11 LR05 RC11 RC06 RC12 RC03; quiz LR18x4 LR17 LR11 LR05 LR19 LR06x2 LR08 LR10 RC11 RC06 RC12 RC03 RC05 RC04.
// Full-length passage (p2): the 1973 Berkeley graduate admissions figures and Simpson's paradox (statistics and social science), written fresh. Facts read 2026-10-17 from Bickel, Hammel and O'Connell, "Sex Bias in Graduate Admissions: Data From Berkeley," Science 187 (1975), 398 to 404 (the PDF via its Wayback copy) and the Wikipedia article "Simpson's paradox": fall 1973, 12,763 applications to 101 departments, 8,442 men and 4,321 women, about 44 and 35 percent admitted; 85 departments analyzed after setting aside 16 (the paper says some had no women applicants or denied no one); four with a deficit of women and six with a deficit of men; the passage softens the English and mechanical engineering shares to "most" and "only a handful" and drops Hammel's dean title (not re-verified at review, 2026-10-17); departments with many women applicants were the harder ones to enter; pooled with the departments taken into account, a small but statistically significant bias in favor of women; the authors trace the pattern to earlier schooling that steered women toward fields that were more crowded, less well funded and with poorer job prospects. Hammel was a professor of anthropology and associate dean of the Graduate Division. Short case passage (p1): Dr. Ingrid Solberg's review letter to Ana Brennan, Monday, July 19, 2027 (new, fiction).
// Test facts read 2026-10-17 on lsac.org: LSATdates lists the June 2027 score release as 6/30/2027, all dates Eastern; the LSAC dates pages say scores are released at 9 a.m. ET through JD Services, which is 7 a.m. in Denver; the LSAT Scoring page says scores need an approved writing sample, come with a score band and a percentile, and arrive with an email. No LSAC wording on the page; the score is said once, a mid 160s number (Anthony's call in the bible).
// Law and practice, story level only: the May 20 order says the parties confer on testing methods (ch8), so Ana confers with Meredith and the district attorney's investigator collects Teo's voluntary reference sample with a chain of custody form. Consensual elimination samples from third parties are ordinary casework; the Colorado procedure for adding one under a postconviction testing order was not verified, so the page claims nothing about it. Teo's legal exposure is never stated: Ana tells him she's his father's lawyer, not his, and gives him a name. His stake is family and conscience.
// Weekdays computed 2026-10-17: Monday, June 14; Wednesdays June 16, June 23 and June 30; Saturday, July 10; Sunday, July 11; Monday, July 12; Friday, July 16; Monday, July 19; Tuesday, July 20; Wednesdays July 21, July 28, August 4, August 11 and August 18, 2027. Saturday, October 27 and Sunday, October 28, 2007.
// Reveal schedule: Teo's whole night (f10s1 to f10s3): the Sprite, the beer, the arm, the first punch, the tailgate, the flashlight above the left eye, his hands wiped on his father's sleeve, Papi; the station by the on ramp at 11:05 and the blue Gatorade (the "Not for gas" echo, and the envelope's ENOUGH, read by the ch2 pick); the flashlight off the bridge over the Platte with Teo watching in the mirror; Abuela's sink and "since supper"; Sunday on the top of the basement stairs as Gene carried the bag up; the viewing words, the first "at Abuela's" since ch5, echoed once in Dani's thought. "You cut his eyebrow" (f10s5). Gene's station question explained (f10s6). Teo's reference sample on Friday, July 16, on his own, still half believing (f10s9). Solberg named, her letter (f10s10). Ana's opinion copy goes back face down; Dani doesn't ask about the circled 1 (ch11 explains it). Luis's no on July 21, set up by his June 9 question (f10s12). Teo tells Ana he'll testify and Dani hears it from Ana (closing). Her chess move on the board at her kitchen table (closing). Kept off the page: Phil's note, the DNA result, her birthday. Luis is never called smaller.
// Flags set here: keptteosecret (f10s8). Read here: crammed (f10s1), calledgenealiar (f10s6), keptteosecret (f10s9), overclaimed (f10s11), pushedluis (f10s12). The ch2 pick at f2s12 (index 2, "somewhere else at 11:15" under ENOUGH) is read in f10s1.
// All questions, passages and answer choices are original. Not affiliated with or endorsed by LSAC.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).lsat=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat.full||[]).push({
  num: 10, part: `Part Three: The Truth`, title: `Non-contributory`, weeks: `June to August 2027`,
  tasks: [`LR05`,`LR06`,`LR08`,`LR10`,`LR11`,`LR17`,`LR18`,`LR19`,`RC03`,`RC04`,`RC05`,`RC06`,`RC11`,`RC12`],
  cast: [
    [`Teo Ruiz`,`Dani's brother, 30. Diesel mechanic at a bus garage. Told her in a parking lot that he hit the man, and then couldn't tell her the rest.`],
    [`Ana Brennan`,`Appellate lawyer on Federal Boulevard. Sent the 2007 autopsy to a pathologist in June and is waiting on the letter.`],
    [`Luis Ruiz`,`Dani's father, 62, at Sterling. Calls Wednesdays at seven. Hasn't asked for her move since May.`],
    [`Bianca Medina`,`Teo's partner and Lupe's mother. Has watched him sit out in the truck every night since June.`],
    [`Dr. Ingrid Solberg`,`Forensic pathologist in Denver. Ana asked her what the 2007 autopsy actually shows.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `In the truck he'd said that much and then couldn't say the rest of it. She waited. Two rows over a woman buckled a kid into a car seat and drove off, and somebody else pulled into the space, and her brother sat with both hands on the wheel of a truck that wasn't going anywhere. After a while he put it in gear. He drove her home on 25 with the radio off and the envelope on the dash, and at her curb he left the engine running.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Not today,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I'll tell you all of it. I can't today."</span> She said okay. It was the one thing she could think of that wouldn't cost him anything. He waited at the curb until she had the front door open, the way their father used to wait when he dropped her at a friend's house, and then he went.`,
    `Monday, June 14, she told Ana at the desk on Federal, standing up, with her bag still on her shoulder. Teo was in the truck when it left the lot. Teo says he hit the man. That's all he's said. Ana didn't write any of it down. She looked out the window at the bus bench across the street for a while and then back at Dani. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then that's what you know,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"When he tells you the rest, I want it the next morning. And you don't interview him. He's your brother. Let him tell it however he tells it."</span>`,
    `Two Wednesdays went by. Her father called at seven both times and talked about the heat on the tier, and about Darnell, who'd finally passed the math part of his GED on the third try and cried, like her father said he would. The board sat on the kitchen table where it had sat since May. It was her move. He didn't ask for it, and she didn't tell him anything about the truck.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ana's Pencil: both at once`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Two true things`,
        body: [`July on Federal. The laundromat runs its dryers with the back door propped open, and the heat comes up through my floor whether I want it or not. Dicta has given up and gone flat on the tile by the bathroom. Pencil. Tonight is the question that hands you two facts and swears both of them are true.`,
          `You've met it since January. Here's the rest of it, because it shows up on nearly every test and often more than once. The passage gives you two facts that look like they can't both hold. A diner on the old highway raised its prices in June and was busier in July than it had ever been. More money and more customers, which is backward from what you'd expect. The stem wants the fact that makes it make sense.`,
          `Rule one is that you believe both facts. The test told you they're true. An answer that says the prices didn't really go up, or that somebody miscounted the crowd, isn't explaining anything. It's arguing with the stem, and the stem wins every time.`,
          `Rule two is that the right answer goes through both facts at once. Say the new interstate exit opened a quarter mile from the diner that same June. Now the crowd has a cause of its own, and the prices stop being strange, because a full room will pay what the menu says. The wrong answers explain one fact and leave the other just as odd as it was. The owner had wanted to raise prices for years? That explains the prices. It says nothing about the crowd.`,
          `Rule three: look for a difference nobody mentioned. Different people, a different stretch of time, something counted two ways. A lot of the paradoxes on this test are two facts about groups that only look like the same group.`],
        terms: [[`Discrepancy`,`Two facts the passage presents as true that seem to conflict. The right answer shows how both can hold.`],[`Reconcile`,`To show how two facts can both be true at once.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask what would most help to explain the findings, resolve the apparent discrepancy, or reconcile two facts. Treat the facts as settled, and test every answer against both of them. If it only touches one, it's gone.` },
      { h: `Can't be`,
        body: [`One of the rarest questions on the test turns the inference questions inside out. The stem asks which answer cannot be true if the passage is. You won't see many, so when you do, read the stem twice. People answer it like must be true and pick the one thing that has to be so, which is the exact opposite of what it asked for.`,
          `The work is must be true run backward. Find what the facts lock in, then look for the answer that contradicts it. The judges at the county fair finished at noon and didn't judge anything after that, and Lena entered her pie at one. An answer that says her pie was judged can't be true. An answer that says she didn't win could easily be true, and so could one that says she'll enter again next year, and neither of those is what the stem wants.`,
          `The traps here are the answers that are only unlikely, or only unproven. Unlikely isn't impossible. If the facts leave even a small door open, the answer could be true, and could be true is the wrong pile.`],
        terms: [[`Must be false`,`An answer that contradicts what the passage establishes, so it can't be true if the passage is.`]],
        exam: `The stem says cannot be true, must be false, or is inconsistent with the statements above. Four answers could be true. One can't.` },
      { h: `The bridge, again`,
        body: [`In December I had you build bridges. A sufficient assumption is the bridge that carries the whole load. Add it to the premises and the conclusion has to follow. Not probably. Has to.`,
          `So find the new thing in the conclusion, the idea the premises never mentioned, and build from what the premises do say to it. A sufficient answer often sounds stronger than you'd want to say out loud. That's fine, because strong is its job. What it can't do is leave a gap, and the usual gap is one the argument papered over with a word like only.`,
          `Two traps. One restates a premise. It feels safe and adds nothing. The other helps, and makes the conclusion more likely, without making it certain. On a necessary assumption question, an answer that falls short of guaranteeing the conclusion can still be right, as long as the argument needs it. Here it's wrong.`],
        terms: [[`Sufficient assumption`,`A premise that, added to the argument, guarantees the conclusion.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which one, if assumed, allows the conclusion to be properly drawn, or makes the conclusion follow logically.` },
      { h: `Before you say it`,
        body: [`An evaluate question asks what you'd most want to know. You learned the test for it in May: answer the question yes, then answer it no, and the right one moves the argument both times.`,
          `There's one more trap I see every summer, the answer that checks a premise. Was the witness's watch right? It feels like good lawyering, and run it both ways. If the watch was wrong, the argument loses its starting point. If the watch was right, you're exactly where you were, still not knowing whether the argument works. That only cuts one way. The best answer usually goes after the step between the premise and the conclusion, not the premise itself.`],
        terms: [[`Gap`,`The step an argument takes from its premises to its conclusion without support.`]],
        exam: `Look for the choice that tests the step, not the starting fact. Ask it yes and ask it no, and keep the one that moves the argument both times.` },
      { h: `Reading an expert`,
        body: [`Reading Comprehension loves an expert's report. A lab sheet, an engineer's letter, a doctor's findings. Four questions keep coming up about them.`,
          `Application asks you to take the passage's own terms and put them on a case it never discusses. Use the passage's definition, not yours. If it uses a word one way, that's the meaning you carry over, even if you'd use the word differently at your own kitchen table.`,
          `Inference asks what the report lets you say that it doesn't say outright. Experts are careful, so the inference is usually small. If a report calls two findings separate, you can say there were two findings. You can't say which came first unless something in the passage keeps time.`,
          `Principle asks what rule the author is working by. Look for the sentence where they tell you how they decided, and it's often near the end. Attitude asks how the author sounds. For a report the answer is usually confident and neutral, or careful. Cold, angry and doubtful are feelings you brought in with you. Check them against the words before you pick one.`],
        terms: [[`Application`,`Using a passage's own terms or standard to judge a case the passage doesn't discuss.`]],
        exam: `Stems ask which situation the author's standard would cover, what can be inferred from the passage, which principle underlies the author's approach, and how the author's attitude is best described.` }
    ],
    video: [`yZDldMokOWc`,`Intro to Paradox Questions | LSAT Logical Reasoning`,`LSAT Demon`,`4:44`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f10s1`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR18`, title:`Not for gas`, q:`What best explains their father tossing out the proof of that stop, then denying the stop for years?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, June 30. At 5:58 the phone lit up on the nightstand.`,
        `<prop type="text" from="Teo" time="5:58 AM">you up</prop>`,
        `She was. She'd been up since four, listening to the sprinklers come on across the street. She wrote back yeah, and he wrote back that Bianca had looked it up and the scores came out at seven, and then nothing, and she pictured him sitting in the truck in his driveway with the phone on his knee.`,
        `At 7:01 the email came. She signed in at the kitchen table with her coffee going cold next to the laptop, and there it was. One sixty five.`,
        ((s.flags.crammed||G.crammed)
          ?`She'd slept two hours the night before the test, and she'd never know what that had cost her, a point or three or nothing at all. She decided at the kitchen table, four minutes after seven, not to try to find out, and she mostly kept to it.`
          :`It was about where her practice tests had said it would land, a point under the last three of them. She looked at it long enough to be sure it wasn't going to change, and then she got up and rinsed the cup out at the sink and set it upside down.`),
        `She texted Teo that it was good.`,
        `<prop type="text" from="Teo" time="7:06 AM">lupe wants to know did you win</prop>`,
        `She told him to tell Lupe yes. Three minutes later he wrote again.`,
        `<prop type="text" from="Teo" time="7:09 AM">saturday the 10th. after shes asleep. ill tell you</prop>`,
        `That night at seven she gave her father the number on the step. He made her say it again, slower, and she did. He didn't ask what it meant, and she didn't tell him about the other text.`,
        `Saturday, July 10, at a quarter to nine, Bianca let her in under the carport. The string lights from March were still up, unplugged. <span class="said" data-who="Bianca">"He's been out in the truck every night since June,"</span> she said, low, at the door. <span class="said" data-who="Bianca">"I'll be in the back with my book. She's asleep. She sleeps like him. Nothing wakes her."</span> Somebody had wiped down the stove and put the dish rack under the sink. Two glasses of water on the table. Down the hall Lupe's door was open about an inch. Her nightlight was a turtle.`,
        `Teo sat down across from her and put his hands flat on the table, like he was about to get up again. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I'm going to go straight through,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Don't stop me, or I won't get back to it."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I begged him,"</span> Teo said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"That's how it starts. Ma's on nights, the Rockies are in the Series for the first time ever, I'm ten. I begged him from Thursday on. Saturday he comes home from the plant and he goes, fine, get your shoes."</span> A table in the back, by the hallway where the bathrooms were. Every time Pa went up to the bar he came back with a Sprite for Teo, and the first couple of times a beer for himself, and Teo kept count. Five Sprites, more than Ma let him have in a week. There was a big guy in a purple Rockies shirt who'd been on them since the third inning.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Last out, I've had like five Sprites, I run for the bathroom. There's a beer on the end of the bar and I hit it with my elbow, and it goes right down the front of the purple shirt guy. He grabs me here."</span> Teo held his own arm above the elbow. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"And he shakes me. I'm ten, my feet are barely on the floor, and he's yelling down in my face, I still don't know what. Then Pa's there. Pa hit him first. I saw it. After that it's legs, all I can see is legs, and Ronnie's yelling for them to take it outside. Pa finds me by the back of my shirt and walks me out the back ahead of him. Nobody ever looked down."</span>`,
        `He told it to the table. Every few sentences he looked down the hall at the crack in Lupe's door.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Out back he puts me in the truck and says stay, and the guy comes out after him and gets him on the tailgate. The flashlight from the plant was behind the seat. I hit him with it, one time."</span> He touched his face. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"He let go and walked off holding his face, bleeding. I wiped my hands on Pa's sleeve. Then I'm crying for him. Papi, Papi."</span>`,
        `Ten to eleven, by a stove clock across the street. Dani held her glass and didn't drink from it.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"He gets me back in the truck and we go. Not home. He goes out 2 and around the long way toward the highway and pulls into a gas station by the on ramp. It's a tire place now. He pumps gas with his sleeve pushed up so I won't look at it, and he comes back with a blue Gatorade for me. I didn't even want it. I held it the whole rest of the night in both hands."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"The clock over the register said 11:05. I'd never been up that late in my life. I looked right at it so I'd remember. And Pa gets his receipt and balls it up and puts it in the can by the pump."</span>`,
        ((((all||{})[2]||{}).picks||{})[11]===2
          ?`Under ENOUGH on the envelope, in her own hand since December, it said somewhere else at 11:15. Her brother had read every line on that envelope in a parking lot in June, and he had no idea he'd just answered one of them.`
          :`She'd drawn a column on that envelope in December and written ENOUGH over it, and asked what would go in it. Somewhere else at a quarter past eleven was the answer, and nobody had ever said where. Her brother had just said where, and he had no idea he'd done it.`),
        `Not for gas, she'd said to her father on the step in January. Straight home, mija. That was a man who'd stood at a pump by the interstate ten minutes before somebody hit Travis Hensley at that curb, with a piece of paper in his hand that had the time on it, and he'd put it in a trash can. A man who didn't do it, throwing away the one thing that said so.`,
        `Her brother was watching her. He'd seen something go across her face and didn't know what.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"He forgot about it, Teo. One stop on a bad night, and by Monday it was gone."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Forgetting doesn't explain January. She'd asked him about gas by name on the step, and the answer came back before she'd finished asking. You have to remember a stop to deny it that fast.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. It denies the problem instead of explaining it. A man who forgot a stop wouldn't deny it the instant he's asked. The fact you need has to explain both the receipt in the can and the denial years later.`},
        {t:`"He didn't know yet what time that man got hit. He threw it out before anybody did."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`That's true of the can, maybe. It isn't true of the step in January. By then he'd sat through six days of trial and knew to the minute when a man got hit at the curb, and he still said straight home.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Half of it. Not knowing the time could explain throwing the receipt out that night. It can't explain years of saying there was no stop, after he knew exactly what that minute was worth.`},
        {t:`"That receipt put Pa at a pump at 11:05, Teo. The camera over the pump had you."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`Teo asks what camera. She tells him that a station with a register has a tape, and a receipt with the time on it is how anybody finds the right minute of the tape. Anybody who went looking for that night would have found a boy in the front seat holding a blue Gatorade in both hands. Teo says he never once thought about the time. He'd looked at the clock so he'd remember being up late. That's all it ever was to him.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Two facts: he didn't strike the man at the curb, and he threw away and then denied the one record that would show it. Both fit if the record proved something else too. The receipt and the station's tape would put his son in the truck, and keeping the boy out of it mattered more to him than his own alibi.`},
        {t:`"He wanted to do the time. He thought he had it coming for the punch in the bar."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Teo says no, flat. Nothing about the way their father has talked all these years sounds like a man who thinks he belongs where he is, and it wouldn't explain throwing the receipt away that same night anyway.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Unsupported. Nothing Luis has said or done points to a man who wanted the sentence, and a wish to be punished doesn't explain why the stop had to disappear.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s2`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR18`, title:`Since supper`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She told him about the curb then, because nobody ever had. A quarter past eleven. A woman across the street at her kitchen window. A man in a red jacket and a ball cap who hit Travis Hensley with something and ran off on foot, and Travis going over backward against the curb. Teo listened with his jaw set.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"He was bleeding all over,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"You didn't see him. He was already going. Whoever that was, he was already going."</span>`,
        `She didn't argue with him. He went on, because he'd said he would.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"He goes the back way to Globeville, and in the middle of the bridge over the Platte he stops. Flashers on. He gets out with the flashlight. I'm watching him in the side mirror. Then he drops it over the rail. I didn't hear it go in. I looked it up once, how deep the Platte is there. Not very."</span>`,
        `So by ten past eleven, give or take, the flashlight was in the Platte, and at a quarter past a man at the curb hit Travis Hensley with something.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Abuela's got the porch light on before we're up the steps. She doesn't ask anything. She takes me to the sink and washes my hands with the dish soap, hot as it goes, and dries them one finger at a time with a dish towel, and gives me a cookie. I didn't eat it. Pa's in the hall with her talking Spanish, low. I heard enough of it. Say he was here since supper. Say he was here all night."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"And she did,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"To Ma. To you. To the priest, probably. To everybody, till she died."</span> He turned his glass a quarter turn on the table. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"She never told a lie in her life, Dani. When I was six she made me walk a candy bar back to the store and say sorry to the man. She went to Mass every morning, and she lied for me for eight years. You know what that means. She knew what I did."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"All she ever lied about was where you were, Teo. A boy in that lot was enough for her."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`Teo says that isn't the same thing. She says it's the whole difference. Abuela's lie was about a place. It didn't need her to know anything about the lot except that a ten year old had been in it, and she'd washed what was on his hands without once asking what it was. He sits with that. He doesn't say she's right, and he doesn't say she's wrong.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Two facts: she never lied, and she lied for him. Teo squares them with the worst thing he can think of. You square them with less. The lie was about where he was, nothing more, and a ten year old in a bar fight is reason enough for a grandmother. It takes no belief about what he did.`},
        {t:`"Pa asked her to. Abuela would have done anything at all Pa ever asked her for, and she did."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I know he asked her,"</span> Teo says. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"That's not what I said."</span> He's right. It explains why she told it. It doesn't touch what he's built on top of it, that a lie that big means a thing that bad.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. True, and only half. Luis asking explains why there was a lie to tell. It doesn't explain why a woman who never lied would tell it, and that's the fact Teo's conclusion rests on.`},
        {t:`"She saw what was on your hands, Teo. She knew right then that man wasn't getting up."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Teo shuts his eyes. She's handed him his own worst version and put Abuela's name on it. Nothing he told her says what their grandmother thought that night. Only what she did.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. It explains her lie by accepting Teo's conclusion, with nothing to support it. Nothing in what he told you says what she knew about the man.`},
        {t:`"Maybe it didn't feel like lying to her. You slept at her house the rest of that night."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Since supper,"</span> Teo says. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"She said since supper, Dani. I was in that bar at supper."</span> A lie with an hour in it is still a lie, and it doesn't answer what he asked her.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. It explains the facts away instead of reconciling them. She said since supper. That wasn't true, whatever it felt like to her.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s3`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR17`, title:`Where`, q:`Which question to Teo would do the most to test what she suspects?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday Abuela drove him home after lunch. Dani was already there. She'd come home from the sleepover in Thornton at eight that morning, and she'd been down to the basement once already and seen the jacket in the washer, and come back up and said nothing to anybody, because Ma was asleep from her shift, and the only other person up was Pa, at the kitchen table, and it was his jacket.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"A guy in a tan coat comes at three,"</span> Teo said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"You take him down the basement. I'm on the top step in my socks. You've got your back to the stairs the whole time. I hear you tell him Ma does the wash on Mondays. Then he comes back up past me with Pa's jacket in a paper bag, and he says excuse me, son. Like I'm in his way."</span>`,
        `She'd stayed down by the washer after, she remembered that, a long while, with her hand on the lid. She'd never known he was on the stairs. Fifteen, and right there, and she'd never once seen him.`,
        `Monday Pa was gone. In June, while she was in Brighton telling a jury about the jacket, Teo stayed with Abuela, and nobody told him anything, and he didn't ask. He worked it out himself from the news on TV and from Ma crying in the car and from what he knew, which was more than anybody in that courtroom.`,
        `Then Ma. The viewing, the hour they gave Pa, the two officers at the back. She knew where she'd been standing. Six feet off, with their aunt's purse on her arm.`,
        `He said it now, in their father's voice, or near it. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"'You were at Abuela's, mijo. You were always at Abuela's.'"</span>`,
        `Nothing else. Over their mother, with his hand on Teo's shoulder.`,
        `Teo was at Abuela's. She'd thought it in Consuelo's kitchen in February, at ten to eleven by the stove clock, like something she knew. Everybody knew where Teo was. Pa had seen to that, and at the casket he'd seen to Teo.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"So that's it,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I hit a man in the head with a steel flashlight, and he died that night, and Pa's done every day of it since. That's what I've got."</span>`,
        `She'd read the report on June 11, the night before the test, at about this hour, and gotten every answer in the margin right. She had the words right there, and she nearly said them, and didn't. Not until she knew they fit what he'd done.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Did Pa ever tell you that you didn't do it, Teo? Even once, in all these years?"`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"He never said anything about it,"</span> Teo says. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Ever."</span> That's a fact about their father. It doesn't tell her whether the page she read in June is about the same blow her brother remembers.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. Whatever Luis did or didn't say, the argument is about where Teo's blow landed and what the report says about that spot. This answer leaves it where it was.`},
        {t:`"How hard did you swing it at him, Teo? With both hands, hard as you could swing?"`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Hard as I could,"</span> he says. It doesn't move anything. The report measured what the flashlight did to the man, and a hard swing that left a shallow cut is still a shallow cut. What she needs is where it landed.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. Close, and it feels important, but the report already says what the blow did. How hard he meant it doesn't turn a superficial cut into anything else.`},
        {t:`"Why didn't you ever tell Bianca any of it? Not even after Lupe came along?"`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Because then she'd know,"</span> he says, and looks down the hall at the crack in Lupe's door. True, and nothing to do with whether the report means what she thinks it does.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. A real question about Teo, and none at all about the argument. Neither answer touches the report or the blow.`},
        {t:`"Where did it hit him, Teo? Show me where on his face it caught him."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:2,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Over the eye,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"The left one."</span> He doesn't have to think about it. If he'd said the back of the head, she'd have had nothing to tell him. He hadn't.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. Your premises: the report calls the wound above the left eye non-contributory and puts the fatal one at the back of the skull, and Teo swung once. Your conclusion: his swing made the cut, not the fracture. The step between them is where the one swing landed. Over the left eye, and the conclusion gets the link it needs. The back of the head, and it falls apart. Either answer moves it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s4`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC11`, title:`Separate`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Somewhere in the telling his hand had gone up to his own face, to the left side above the eyebrow, two fingers, like you'd touch a bruise to see if it was still there. She didn't think he knew he did it. He'd probably been doing it since he was ten.`,
        `She could see the page. She'd read it like Ana told her to, like a passage, and it was all still there.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="What she remembered of the report, word for word">Laceration, 2.5 cm, above the left eyebrow. Superficial. Does not reach the bone.<br>Depressed fracture of the back of the skull.<br>The fatal injury is the depressed fracture at the back of the skull. The laceration above the left eyebrow is a separate, superficial injury and is non-contributory.</prop>`,
        `Ana's rule for this kind was always the same. Use the page's words, not yours, and put them on what he did.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It was the first of the two blows that night, and the same man landed both of them.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The report never says how many men, or which came first. Separate is all it says. She'd be putting a man in it that the page doesn't have.`,
          why:`Application. Not in the passage. The report says the injuries were separate. It doesn't count blows, order them, or say who struck either one.`},
        {t:`It cut him above the left eye, and that cut had no part at all in why he died.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:4},
          after:`That's the word, set on his swing. It was a real cut. It bled, and cuts there do. It had nothing to do with how the night ended. She has it right, in the page's own words.`,
          why:`Application. Take the report's own terms and put them on Teo's swing. A superficial laceration above the left eyebrow, separate from the fatal fracture and non-contributory, means a real injury that played no part in the death. That's the swing, described by the page.`},
        {t:`It hurt him a good deal less than it looked to a boy of ten in the dark.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Superficial, yes. But less than it looked isn't what the page is about. The page is about what killed him, and it's careful to say the cut didn't.`,
          why:`Application. Partly right. Superficial does describe the cut. But the passage's point about it is that it didn't contribute to the death, and this answer leaves that out.`},
        {t:`It's what knocked him back down against the curb, and the fall did all the rest.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The report doesn't say what put him on the curb. It says the fracture at the back killed him and the cut in front is a separate injury. Putting the fall on his swing is the one thing the page never does.`,
          why:`Application. Unsupported. The report says the cut played no part in the death. It never says what put him on the curb, so its words can't be carried over to say the swing caused the fall.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s5`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR11`, title:`The once`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She kept her voice down, for Lupe. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"You cut his eyebrow."</span>`,
        `He looked at her. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"It's in the autopsy. Ana gave it to me in June. There's two injuries on page one, Teo. The back of his head is what killed him. Over his eye, it didn't even get to the bone. The doctor's got a word for that."</span> She said it like it was typed. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Non-contributory."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"That's a piece of paper,"</span> he said, after a while. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I was there. He was bleeding all down his shirt."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"A cut there bleeds like that. And at four minutes after eleven he was on the phone to his sister. Talking. Twenty minutes after you."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"People talk when they're dying. You see it on TV."</span>`,
        `He wasn't going to put it down in one night, she could see that. He'd been carrying it since he was ten.`,
        `But there were two things on the table now, his and the report's, and if both of them were right, then one thing about that night couldn't be. She knew which one. She had to say it so he'd hear it as a fact, and not as his sister being kind.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"Then somebody else hit him after you did, Teo, at the curb, and that's the only way it works."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Maybe somebody did. Consuelo saw somebody. But a man can go over backward against a curb with nobody's help, and the report doesn't say which. What has to be false is narrower, and she went past it to the one he can argue with. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"You don't know that,"</span> he says, and he's right.`,
          why:`Must be false. It goes too far the other way. Both accounts leave room for a second attacker, and Consuelo saw one, but neither requires it. He could have fallen. A must be false question wants the claim the facts rule out, not one they merely suggest.`},
        {t:`"Then that cut barely hurt him, Teo. It only looked that bad in the dark out there in the lot."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Teo stares at her. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"He was bleeding through his fingers."</span> The report says superficial, not small. A cut can be shallow and still bleed like that. She's said a thing the page doesn't, and he caught it.`,
          why:`Must be false. Not what the facts rule out. Superficial means shallow, not painless, and the report never says how much the cut hurt. It says the fracture was fatal.`},
        {t:`"Then it can't be true that your swing killed him. One swing, there, and he walked off."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`Teo says she doesn't know that. She says she knows what the report says and what he just told her, and those two can't both be right if his swing is what killed Travis Hensley. He gets up and goes down the hall and stands in Lupe's door a while with his back to the kitchen. When he comes back he says, <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"I don't know,"</span> and sits down, and she lets that be enough for one night.`,
          why:`Must be false. Teo's account: one swing, to the brow, and the man let go and walked off holding his face. The report: the brow wound is separate and non-contributory, and the fatal injury is at the back of the skull. If both are right, all his swing did was a cut that played no part in the death, so the one claim that can't be true is that his swing caused it. Everything else is still open.`},
        {t:`"Then Pa knew the whole time you didn't do it, and he let you go on thinking you had."`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Teo's face closes. Maybe it's even true. It isn't something his account and the report make impossible, or anything they say at all, and now he's thinking about their father instead of the cut.`,
          why:`Must be false. Neither account says anything about what Luis knew or believed. A claim the facts don't touch can't be one they make impossible.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s6`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR18`, title:`Anybody else`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She drove home at midnight down 104th with the windows open and the radio off. The envelope was in the prep book on the passenger seat, and she didn't take it out till she was home.`,
        `At the kitchen table she took the envelope out after all. Under ENOUGH, small, in pencil, she wrote 11:05, A PUMP BY THE ON RAMP. She left the top line where it was, in her own capitals from December. A pencil hadn't put it there, and a pencil wasn't going to take it off.`,
        `She didn't need Gene's notebook for the rest. She had his voice. Monday night, six hours in a room, and her father had asked him one thing. Was anybody else hurt?`,
        ((s.flags.calledgenealiar||G.calledgenealiar)
          ?`She'd called him a liar in his own garage in December, and in January he'd told her anyway, sitting on the stool by the covered car, like a man setting something down on a bench before he left it there.`
          :`He'd told her at the end, sitting on the stool by the covered car, and said he'd quit thinking about it.`),
        `Strange thing for a guilty man to ask, Gene had said. Strange for an innocent one too. In January she'd sat in his drive with the engine running and counted her family. Her in Thornton. Mom at work. Teo with Abuela. There wasn't anybody else.`,
        `There was. He'd been in the lot.`,
        `So her father hadn't done it, and the one thing he'd asked the man who arrested him was the thing Gene couldn't make fit. She sat with it under the lamp and tried to make it fit.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`He'd been in that lot for the start of it. He'd left before the end and didn't know what came after.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Anybody else. A guilty man knows who got hurt, because he did it. A man who was never there doesn't think anybody was. Her father had watched Travis go off across the lot holding his face, and driven away, and on Monday night somebody told him the man was dead at a curb. He was asking what happened after he left. Gene had been right that it was strange. It was only strange for the man Gene thought he had.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Gene's puzzle was a question that fit neither a guilty man nor an innocent one. It fits a third kind of man: one who saw the fight begin, saw Travis walk off hurt, and left before the end. He knows there was violence, and he doesn't know who else it reached.`},
        {t:`He was afraid for Teo. Travis had grabbed the boy by the arm in the bar in front of the whole room.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`He was. But Teo was the one person Pa didn't need to ask about. He'd had him in the truck and left him at his mother's sink. Fear for the boy doesn't explain asking a detective about anybody else.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Half of it. Fear for Teo is real, but Luis knew exactly where his son was. It doesn't explain a question about anybody else.`},
        {t:`He was asking about Ronnie. Ronnie got in between the two of them inside and might have taken a punch.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ronnie testified at the trial, unhurt, and nobody ever said he took a punch. It explains a question her father might have asked. It doesn't explain this one.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Unsupported. Nothing in the record says Ronnie was hurt or that Luis worried he was.`},
        {t:`He was guilty, and he asked it to sound like he wasn't. Gene was right to quit thinking about it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She writes it down and looks at it and can't keep it. Not since 11:05. If she's taking Teo's night as true, and she is, then guilty is the one thing this question can't be explained by.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. It throws out one of the facts. A paradox answer has to let both facts stand, and here one of them is that Luis didn't do it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s7`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC06`, title:`How far apart`, q:`From that page alone, what can she properly infer?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At one in the morning she had the report out of the folder and flat on the table under the lamp, the copy Ana gave her in June, eight pages stapled at the corner. She read the last paragraph again, the 2007 doctor's. Then the page with the two injuries on it, one above the other.`,
        `Teo needed an order. A blow, then a man walking off, then a curb. The State had needed an order too, the other way around. She wanted to know how much of anybody's order was on this page, and how much she'd be bringing to it.`,
        `And the cuff. The blood on it was the third thing the court had leaned on in 2010, after the jacket and the fight. Some of what was on that sleeve had come off a ten year old's hands before ten to eleven, twenty five minutes before anybody was at the curb.`,
        `Line two on Ana's board said the attack or the bar. Some of it was neither.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That the cut over his eye came first, and the fracture at the back came later on.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`She wants it to say that. It doesn't. First and later are words for a clock, and the report is about a body. The order comes from Teo and from a phone call, not from page one.`,
          why:`Inference. Unsupported by the report. It may well be true, but the order comes from Teo's account and the timeline, not from this page.`},
        {t:`That a single blow made both injuries, the way one swing can mark two places.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Separate, the report says. Two injuries, front and back, and it keeps them apart on purpose. One swing doesn't fit the word.`,
          why:`Inference. Contradicted. The report calls the brow wound a separate injury.`},
        {t:`That he died within a few minutes of the blow to the back of his head.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The first page says he died at the hospital at 1:40 in the morning, after treatment. That's hours, not minutes.`,
          why:`Inference. Contradicted. Death was pronounced at 1:40 AM after treatment, well after any blow that night.`},
        {t:`That they were two separate injuries. It never says how far apart they came.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Separate, and that's all. The page doesn't keep time. She turns her card over and writes the times down from where they came from. Ten to eleven, the boy crying, by Consuelo's stove. 11:04, the call to Shannon. 11:05, the register over Teo's Gatorade. 11:15, Consuelo at her window again. The report isn't in any of them. Every clock that night belonged to somebody else, and the one Teo read, he read at ten.`,
          why:`Inference. The report calls the injuries separate, which means two distinct injuries, not one. It says nothing about when either happened, so you can't infer how much time passed between them. The order has to come from the witnesses.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s8`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR17`, title:`The next morning`, q:`Which would be most useful to know in testing her card?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday, July 11. She found the corner on the map on her phone at seven, before coffee. Teo had said by the on ramp, and there was only one on ramp it could be. It was a tire shop now, like he'd said. Four miles from where the Night Owl had stood.`,
        `When he tells you the rest, I want it the next morning. It was the next morning, and a Sunday. Monday at seven, then, before her bus. That gave her a day to try to break it first.`,
        `She wrote it on a card first, as Ana would have made her. PA AT A PUMP FOUR MILES OFF AT 11:05. SO PA WASN'T THE MAN AT THE CURB AT 11:15. If she walked in on Federal with an argument, it had better be one she'd already tried to break.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Whether Pa paid for the gas and the Gatorade in cash, or with a card that had his name on it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Cash or card might change what a records search could find twenty years later, which is nothing anyway. It doesn't change whether the argument works. Monday morning she tells Ana all of it, the card and the tire shop and the blue Gatorade, and Ana asks the question she didn't.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. Irrelevant to the reasoning. How he paid says nothing about whether a man at that pump at 11:05 could be at the curb at 11:15.`},
        {t:`Whether a man could drive those four miles back to the lot before 11:15.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:4},
          after:`She maps it back the other way. Eleven minutes at night, the phone says, if every light goes green. Ten minutes on one clock and eleven on the road, and nobody ever checked the station's clock against Consuelo's. Too close to carry it alone, and she's glad she found that out before Ana did. Then the rest of it, which only Teo had. The pump, then the bridge, then Abuela's porch, and Teo in the cab for all of it. The man at the curb ran off on foot. She puts it on the card, and next to it, small, TEO'S WORD. Ana would ask. At nine she texts Teo that she has to tell Ana tomorrow, and he writes back i know. Monday at seven she tells Ana all of it, in order, standing at the desk.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. The argument moves from four miles away at 11:05 to not at the curb at 11:15. Ask whether he could get back in time. If he could, the pump doesn't settle it and you need more. If he couldn't, the argument holds. Either answer changes it.`},
        {t:`Whether a boy of ten could have read the register clock right that late at night.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`She could ask him. He'd say he's sure, and he would be. If he read it wrong, the argument loses where it starts. If he read it right, she still doesn't know whether it holds, so the question only cuts one way. She texts Teo that she has to tell Ana, and he writes back i know. Monday she tells Ana everything.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. It only cuts one way. If Teo misread the clock, the argument loses its premise. If he read it right, you still don't know whether ten minutes was enough, which is the gap.`},
        {t:`Nothing she can answer on a Sunday. She keeps all of it to herself until she can.`, s:0, flag:`keptteosecret`, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`She tells herself it's Teo's to tell and not hers, and that Ana would want it all checked first anyway. She doesn't call on Monday. Tuesday she sits at her desk at work with her phone face down next to the keyboard. Wednesday at seven her father calls and talks about the heat, and she says nothing to him either. By Friday it's been six days.`,
          why:`Evaluate the argument. Not an answer, and the wrong call. You don't evaluate an argument by sitting on it. Ana asked for it the next morning, and she's the one who'd have asked the question you skipped.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s9`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC03`, title:`Face down`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[...((s.flags.keptteosecret||G.keptteosecret)
          ?[`Friday, July 16, at a quarter to nine, Ana called her at work, and Dani took it in the copy room with the door shut. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Your brother came up my stairs at eight this morning in his work shirt,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He told me a story. I think you've had it since Saturday."</span> Dani said yes. Ana didn't say anything. On Ana's end the dryers were going. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Tuesday,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Bring my opinion back."</span> Then she was gone.`]
          :[`On Monday Ana had written every line of it down in pencil, read it back to her, and changed nothing. Friday, July 16, at twenty to seven in the evening, Bianca texted.`,
            `<prop type="text" from="Bianca" time="6:40 PM">he went to anas this morning. didnt tell me till tonight. he says tell dani its done</prop>`]),
        `He'd gone up the stairs on Federal by himself. Ana told it to her plainly on Tuesday, the way she told everything. He'd sat in the chair and asked what it would take for them to have his DNA. She'd told him she was his father's lawyer and not his, that anything he said to her she might have to use, and that he should talk to a lawyer of his own first. She wrote him a name. He said he'd heard her. Then Ana called Meredith, because the order said the parties would confer on the testing, and conferring was what they did. At three that afternoon an investigator from the district attorney's office swabbed the inside of his cheek at Ana's desk and sealed it in front of both of them, and they both signed the form.`,
        `Teo asked one thing before he went back to work. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"If it's me under there, it says so?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It says whatever's there,"</span> Ana told him, and he said okay.`,
        `So he'd done it still thinking it might be him. Dani hadn't been in the room for any of it.`,
        `Tuesday, July 20, she brought the opinion back. The coffee ring on the back, the small circled 1 in the margin of page eleven beside the one sentence. Ana took it without looking at it. She opened the top drawer and laid it in face down and shut the drawer. Dani didn't ask about the 1. She'd decided that on the bus.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She wrote,"</span> Ana said, and put a single page on the desk, face up, with a paper clip at the top. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Dr. Solberg. It came yesterday. Before you read it, read 2007 one more time. Not what it says. How it talks."</span> She slid the old report over beside the letter, open to its last paragraph.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Cold. It writes about a man, and his injuries, like a part that failed.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Clinical,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Cold is a feeling, and that one's yours."</span> The page describes. It doesn't look down on him. A report that lists injuries isn't saying anything about the man who had them.`,
          why:`Author's attitude. Half right. The report is detached, but detached isn't cold. Nothing in it shows contempt or indifference toward the man.`},
        {t:`Unsure of itself. It hedges on which injury was the one that killed him.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Where's the hedge?"</span> Ana says. There isn't one. The fatal injury is the fracture, it says, flat, and the cut is non-contributory. Nothing in it says maybe.`,
          why:`Author's attitude. Contradicted. It says plainly which injury was fatal.`},
        {t:`Plain and certain. It names each injury, then which one killed him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Sure about what it looked at,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And not one word past it. No who, no when, no how sorry anybody is."</span> She taps the words fatal injury. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's somebody who's done a lot of these and doesn't need anybody to like the report."</span>`,
          why:`Author's attitude. The report states its findings without qualification and ranks the injuries, one fatal and one non-contributory. That's confident and neutral, and it never goes past the body.`},
        {t:`Wary of the police. It makes a point of who took the scrapings away.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It says released to Detective Vigil,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's a chain of custody line. Every report has one. Nobody's being wary of anybody."</span>`,
          why:`Author's attitude. Unsupported. Noting who received the scrapings is routine, not suspicion.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s10`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC12`, title:`Where they stop`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Then the letter.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Ingrid Solberg, M.D., forensic pathology, Denver. Letter to Ana Brennan, Monday, July 19, 2027">You asked me to review the 2007 report of autopsy on Travis Hensley and to tell you what its findings establish. I have read the report, the fourteen photographs taken at autopsy, and the hospital record.<br>I agree with the original pathologist on the cause of death. The fatal injury is the depressed fracture at the back of the skull. Its features are consistent with a blow from an object or with the back of the head striking a hard edge, such as a curb. The findings alone don't tell me which.<br>The laceration above the left eyebrow is superficial and does not involve the bone. A wound there bleeds freely and can look far worse than it is. It would not have caused death, and in my opinion it did not contribute to it, which is what the original report means by non-contributory.<br>I can't tell from the record which injury came first or how much time separated them. He lived for some hours after he reached the hospital, so the bruising doesn't settle the order.<br>I have kept this opinion to what the record shows. For anything more than that you'll need a witness. The body won't tell you.</prop>`,
        `Dani read it twice. Ana waited, and drank her coffee, and let her.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Five paragraphs, and in the last one she tells you how she wrote the other four,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"What's the rule she's working by?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Say only what the findings show and where the findings stop.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the whole letter,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She agrees where she can, she won't guess at the order, and she tells me so in writing. A judge will believe every word of it for exactly that reason."</span> She puts the letter in the folder, on top.`,
          why:`Principle. Solberg states it herself: she has kept her opinion to what the record shows. She agrees where the findings support it, declines to say which injury came first, and says where the evidence stops.`},
        {t:`When the evidence is unclear, lean toward the man who was convicted.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Find where she leans,"</span> Ana says. Dani can't. Solberg agrees with 2007 on the cause of death and refuses to guess at the order, which helps nobody's side in particular.`,
          why:`Principle. Unsupported. Nothing in the letter favors the defendant. It confirms the State's cause of death.`},
        {t:`A second opinion should agree with the first unless it finds a mistake.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`She does agree, Ana says, on the cause. That's an outcome. The rule is in the last paragraph, and it would have had her disagree just as plainly if the photographs had shown something else.`,
          why:`Principle. It describes a result, not the rule behind it. She agrees with the first report on cause, but her stated principle is to say only what the record shows, which could have led her to disagree.`},
        {t:`A reviewer should go past the first report, and fill in what it left out.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She did the opposite,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Read her fourth paragraph. On the order of things she went exactly as far as 2007 did, which is nowhere."</span>`,
          why:`Principle. Contradicted. She declines to go beyond the record on timing, the one place where she could have reached further.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR05`, title:`What's missing`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana took a card out of the box and wrote on it in capitals, slowly, the way she wrote the State's arguments. THE FRACTURE KILLED HIM. THE CUT DIDN'T. TEO MADE THE CUT. SO TEO DIDN'T KILL HIM.`,
        ((s.flags.overclaimed||G.overclaimed)
          ?`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"No proves anywhere on it,"</span> she said, without looking up. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You'll notice."</span> She turned it around.`
          :`She turned it around.`),
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Your brother's going to read this someday, one way or another,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"So it had better be true all the way down. What do you add to it to make the last line have to follow?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That the 2007 pathologist was right about which of the two injuries killed him.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's already on the card,"</span> Ana says. Line one. Saying it twice doesn't close anything. The gap is between the cut and everything else his swing might have done.`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. Already a premise. Restating that the fracture was fatal doesn't connect Teo's swing to anything new.`},
        {t:`That Teo swung the flashlight at him only the one time, and only at his face.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"One time,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"One time is plenty to put a man down on a curb. You've counted the swings. You haven't said what the one swing did."</span>`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. Close, and not enough. One swing at the face could still have caused a fall onto the back of the head. The number of swings isn't the gap.`},
        {t:`That somebody else was in that lot at a quarter past eleven.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Maybe,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Consuelo says so. But a second man doesn't make the last line follow. Your brother could still have done the damage, and the second man could have come along after."</span>`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. It makes the conclusion more likely, but doesn't make it follow. Another man in the lot is consistent with Teo's swing having done the fatal damage.`},
        {t:`That the cut over his eye is the only harm Teo ever did to him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the piece,"</span> Ana says. She writes it in small between lines three and four. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And it isn't a medical question. Solberg can't give it to you. A cut doesn't say whether the man fell down afterward."</span> She taps the card. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Your brother can say he walked off. And a phone call at 11:04 says he was still talking twenty minutes later."</span>`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. The premises say the cut wasn't fatal and Teo made it. To guarantee he didn't kill Travis, you need the cut to be everything his swing did: no fall it caused, no second injury. Add that, and the conclusion has to follow.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f10s12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR18`, title:`Ten years`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana had called her father on Friday afternoon, after the swab, a legal call, the little room with the phone on the wall. She'd told him all of it, she said, because he was her client and it was his case, and because otherwise he'd hear it from somebody worse.`,
        `Wednesday, July 21, seven. Her step. The recording, and then him, and no hello. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Brennan called."</span>`,
        `She waited. Behind him somebody was yelling about a card game. In June he'd asked her what she and her brother talked about, all that way in a truck, and she'd told him nothing, same as always. She heard the question now for what it had been.`,
        ((s.flags.pushedluis||G.pushedluis)
          ?`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"You asked me at that table where I really went,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"So now you know where. You and Brennan and some investigator with a cotton swab."</span> It was the voice from the end of the visit, after he'd quit looking at her.`
          :`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He was ten years old,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Ten."</span>`),
        `<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Ten years he won't come up here,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Not a card at Christmas. Not once on the phone. And now he walks up Brennan's stairs by himself and gives them his spit in a tube."</span> A breath. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He doesn't testify. I told Brennan and I'm telling you. He doesn't get up there."</span>`,
        `Ten years of nothing and then that. He wanted somebody to tell him how it was the same boy.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"He's been angry at you from the day Ma died, Pa. That's why he never once came up."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Angry,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Angry people write you a letter and tell you so."</span> Maybe he's right. Angry explains ten years away. It doesn't explain a Friday morning at Brennan's, and he knows it doesn't, and so does she.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Half of it. Anger could explain the ten years. It does nothing to explain why he'd now volunteer his DNA for his father's case.`},
        {t:`"At Ma's casket you told him where he'd been, Pa. He kept it ten years. Then he told me."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:2,health:4},
          after:`He doesn't say anything right away. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He told you that."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"He told me all of it, Pa."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Not on this phone, mija."</span> She hears him breathe out. Ten years of a boy keeping what his father told him over his mother's casket, and one Friday on Federal when he quit keeping it. One answer covers both. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"He doesn't testify,"</span> her father says again, quieter.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. The two facts are ten years of staying away and a sudden walk up Ana's stairs. One cause explains both. At the casket Luis told Teo where the story put him, and staying away was keeping it. Once he'd told his sister, there was nothing left to keep.`},
        {t:`"Bianca's been after him for years to do something about you, Pa. She finally won."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I never met Bianca,"</span> he says. That's true, and it's the end of that. Nobody put Teo on those stairs but Teo, and she's guessing at a woman to keep from saying the thing she knows.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. Unsupported. Nothing suggests Bianca sent him, and it explains neither the ten years nor the timing.`},
        {t:`"The DNA's coming back, Pa. He wants to be on the right side of it when it does."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"The right side,"</span> he says, slowly. It makes her brother sound like a man placing a bet. Teo walked up those stairs thinking it might be his own skin under there. That isn't somebody picking a side.`,
          why:`Resolve the paradox. It explains the visit to Ana, badly, and not the ten years. And it doesn't fit what you know: he went believing the result might be against him.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f10s2`, title:`What explains both`, intro:`Ana's index cards, the blue ones she keeps for summer. Each card has two facts that seem to fight. Pick the one that lets both of them be true.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Card one. The town planted a hundred trees along Main Street, which took out a dozen parking spaces, and the next summer the shops there had more customers than ever.`, choices:[`The trees blocked some of the parking signs.`,`Most of the new customers walked over from the apartments behind Main Street, now that the walk was shaded.`,`Parking downtown has always been hard to find.`], answer:1, why:`Fewer spaces and more customers fit together if the new customers didn't need a space. Blocked signs would only make parking harder, and parking that was always hard explains neither change.`},
      {prompt:`Card two. A high school's graduation rate went up the same year its average test scores went down.`, choices:[`The state made the test harder that year.`,`Several of the school's teachers retired.`,`A new program kept students who'd been about to drop out in school through graduation.`], answer:2, why:`Students who'd have left now stay, so more graduate, and their scores pull the average down. A harder test explains the scores only.`},
      {prompt:`Card three. Ana's sister swears by an umbrella brand that she says never breaks, and she buys a new one every spring.`, choices:[`She keeps leaving them on the bus.`,`The brand raised its prices this year.`,`She tells everybody at work to buy that brand.`], answer:0, why:`An umbrella can be unbreakable and still get lost. Telling people about the brand explains why she likes it, not why she needs a new one every year, and a price rise explains neither.`},
      {prompt:`Card four. A clinic hired a second doctor, and the average wait to see a doctor there got longer.`, choices:[`The waiting room was repainted the same month.`,`Word got around, and three times as many patients started coming.`,`The new doctor takes a little longer with each patient.`], answer:1, why:`Three times the patients will swamp twice the doctors. A second doctor, even a slow one, still sees patients the clinic couldn't see before, so on its own the wait should get shorter, and the paint has nothing to do with it.`},
      {prompt:`Card five. Fewer people die in car crashes in the county than did ten years ago, though there are more cars on its roads.`, choices:[`The county's population grew.`,`Some drivers still don't wear seat belts.`,`Cars now come with air bags and better brakes.`], answer:2, why:`Safer cars let more cars and fewer deaths happen together. Population growth explains the cars, not the deaths, and seat belts don't explain a drop.`},
      {prompt:`Card six. Monday's lunch special sells out every week, though the cook calls it the worst thing on the menu.`, choices:[`It's the cheapest plate on the menu, and there are only twenty of them.`,`The cook is hard on every dish in the kitchen.`,`Monday is the busiest lunch of the week.`], answer:0, why:`Cheap and scarce sells out whatever the cook thinks of it. A cook who's hard on every dish explains the opinion only, and a busy Monday doesn't explain why this dish goes first.`}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`Dr. Ingrid Solberg's letter to Ana Brennan, Monday, July 19, 2027`,
      text:[`You asked me to review the 2007 report of autopsy on Travis Hensley and to tell you what its findings establish. I have read the report, the fourteen photographs taken at autopsy, and the hospital record.`,
        `I agree with the original pathologist on the cause of death. The fatal injury is the depressed fracture at the back of the skull. Its features are consistent with a blow from an object or with the back of the head striking a hard edge, such as a curb. The findings alone don't tell me which.`,
        `The laceration above the left eyebrow is superficial and does not involve the bone. A wound there bleeds freely and can look far worse than it is. It would not have caused death, and in my opinion it did not contribute to it, which is what the original report means by non-contributory.`,
        `I can't tell from the record which injury came first or how much time separated them. He lived for some hours after he reached the hospital, so the bruising doesn't settle the order.`,
        `I have kept this opinion to what the record shows. For anything more than that you'll need a witness. The body won't tell you.`] },
    p2: { title:`Dani's practice set: admissions at Berkeley, 1973`,
      text:[`In the fall of 1973 the graduate division of the University of California at Berkeley had 12,763 applications complete enough to decide, from 8,442 men and 4,321 women. About 44 percent of the men were admitted and about 35 percent of the women. A gap that size, across that many people, is very unlikely to happen by chance, and the university had good reason to worry that it was looking at bias against women.`,
        `Three people at Berkeley looked closer: the statistician Peter Bickel, Eugene Hammel and J. W. O'Connell. What they published in Science in 1975 is still taught as a warning. Admission at Berkeley wasn't decided by the university as a whole. Each department decided on the people who applied to it. So they set the campus totals aside and went department by department, eighty five of them once they set aside the sixteen that had no women applicants or turned nobody away. Most showed no sign of bias either way. In four, fewer women were admitted than the department's own rate would predict. In six, it was the men who came out behind.`,
        `How can the parts look fair, or close to it, while the whole looks unfair? The answer was in where people applied. Men and women hadn't applied to the same departments. Most of the applicants to English were women, and only a handful of the applicants to mechanical engineering were. The departments that drew many women tended to be crowded ones that turned most people away, men and women alike, and the departments that drew mostly men tended to admit a much bigger share of whoever applied. Women were rejected more often on the whole because they were applying more often to the departments that rejected nearly everybody.`,
        `When the researchers combined the departments in a way that took this into account, the gap against women was gone, and what was left was a small but statistically significant bias in their favor. Statisticians already had a name for that kind of reversal, Simpson's paradox, and Berkeley became its best known example.`,
        `The authors didn't stop at the arithmetic, and their last point is the one most often dropped when the case gets retold. If the admissions committees weren't the source of the gap, something earlier was. Women, they wrote, had been steered by how they'd been raised and schooled toward fields that were more crowded and less well funded, with worse job prospects at the end. A department by department count can clear a committee. It can't tell you why the applicants lined up the way they did, and the authors never claimed it could.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`Harlow County's libraries stopped charging late fees last year. Since then, the number of books returned late has risen by nearly a third. Yet over the same period, the number of books that were never returned at all fell by more than half.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy described above?`,
      opts:[`Most of the libraries' borrowers live within two miles of a branch library.`,`The libraries opened several new branches during that same year.`,`The late fees had been raised twice during the previous ten years.`,`Many borrowers had kept overdue books for good rather than face a fee.`,`Books returned late are often in worse shape than books returned on time.`],
      a:3, why:`Resolve the paradox. If people used to keep overdue books to avoid paying, dropping the fee lets those books come back, late. More late returns and fewer missing books both follow. Distance and new branches don't explain both changes, past fee increases describe the old policy without explaining the shift, and the condition of late books is beside the point.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Anglers had fished Pell Lake for decades when, two years ago, the state banned fishing there to protect its trout. Surveys since then show that the lake's trout population has fallen sharply, while trout in nearby lakes where fishing is still allowed have held steady.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain the findings?`,
      opts:[`Pell Lake is deeper and colder than most of the lakes around it.`,`Most fish taken from Pell Lake had been pike, which feed on young trout.`,`Anglers at the nearby lakes release most of the trout that they catch.`,`The trout in Pell Lake are the same species as those in nearby lakes.`,`Several anglers were fined last year for fishing at Pell Lake anyway.`],
      a:1, why:`Resolve the paradox. If anglers mostly removed pike, the ban left more pike eating young trout, which explains a drop at Pell Lake alone. Depth and cold don't explain a change, release at the other lakes explains why they held steady but not why Pell fell, the species is the same, and a few fines don't account for the decline.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`A fire department survey of homeowners in Varden found that those who own at least one smoke detector are more likely to say they've had a house fire than those who own none. The department had expected the survey to show the opposite.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to explain this finding?`,
      opts:[`Smoke detectors are sold at nearly every hardware store in Varden.`,`Some homeowners don't test their smoke detectors as often as they should.`,`Fires that start in kitchens are the most common kind in Varden.`,`Homeowners who've had a fire are more willing than others to answer surveys.`,`Many Varden homeowners buy a smoke detector only after they've had a fire.`],
      a:4, why:`Resolve the paradox. If people tend to buy a detector after a fire, the fires came first, and owners will report more of them. That explains the link without detectors causing anything. Where they're sold, testing habits and kitchen fires don't explain it, and a survey that draws people who've had fires would raise both groups alike.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Since hikers in the Callan range began carrying phones that can show their location, they get lost far less often than they used to. Yet over the same years, the number of rescue calls from hikers in the range has gone up.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?`,
      opts:[`Hikers now call for help with blisters and sprains they once walked out on.`,`The phones work in most of the range but not in a few of its deepest canyons.`,`Hikers in the range have long been advised to tell someone where they're going.`,`The range's rescue teams have added volunteers and a second truck in recent years.`,`Some hikers in the range still don't carry a phone when they go out on the trails.`],
      a:0, why:`Resolve the paradox. If hikers now call over injuries they used to handle themselves, rescue calls can rise while getting lost falls. Dead spots and hikers without phones don't explain a rise. Old advice and bigger rescue teams don't explain why the calls went up.`, task:`LR18`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Basketball coach: Halfway through the season I added a drill in which players shoot free throws with their eyes closed. The players grumbled about it at first. Since then the team's free throw percentage has risen from 68 percent to 75 percent. The drill is working.<br><br>Which one of the following would be most useful to know in evaluating the coach's argument?`,
      opts:[`whether the team's opponents have also improved at free throws this season`,`whether the drill was first used by a coach at another school in the league`,`whether players who skipped the drill improved as much as those who did it`,`whether the team makes a higher share of its free throws than of its other shots`,`whether the players enjoy the new drill more than the ones they did before it`],
      a:2, why:`Evaluate the argument. If players who skipped the drill improved just as much, something else, like a season of practice, explains the rise. If only the drill players improved, the drill looks like the cause. Either answer moves the argument. Opponents improving too would point only weakly to something league wide, and where the drill came from, other shots and enjoyment don't bear on it at all.`, task:`LR17`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`,`STAT`]},
    {q:`Every painting moved out of the museum's east gallery last week was moved after the gallery closed at 5:00 PM. The Halloran landscape was moved out of the east gallery last week. Dorsey and Mehta, the museum's two art handlers, were not in the building after 4:30 PM on any day last week.<br><br>If the statements above are true, which one of the following cannot be true?`,
      opts:[`The Halloran landscape was moved after 5:00 PM on one day last week.`,`Dorsey and Mehta worked in the east gallery on several days last week.`,`Someone other than the art handlers moved a painting last week.`,`The east gallery closed at 5:00 PM on every day of last week.`,`Dorsey and Mehta moved the Halloran landscape out of the gallery.`],
      a:4, why:`Must be false. The Halloran was moved last week, so it was moved after 5:00. Dorsey and Mehta were gone by 4:30 every day, so they can't have moved it. Moving it after 5 and someone else moving a painting both have to be true, and the other two choices could be.`, task:`LR11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Teachers at Roan School may chaperone the spring field trip only if they have completed first aid training during the current school year, and only the district's own courses count. Mr. Abiola last completed first aid training during the previous school year. This year the district is offering two courses: a full course held in the fall, which he missed while on leave, and a single refresher course in the spring, which he has already signed up to take. So Mr. Abiola cannot chaperone the spring field trip.<br><br>The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?`,
      opts:[`The spring field trip will take place before this year's refresher course is held.`,`Most teachers who missed the fall course this year have signed up for the refresher.`,`A refresher course doesn't cover everything that the full first aid course covers.`,`Mr. Abiola has never chaperoned a field trip for the school in any previous year.`,`Teachers on leave aren't allowed to sign up for district courses held that fall.`],
      a:0, why:`Sufficient assumption. He can go only with training completed this school year, and the only training left to him is the spring refresher. If the trip comes before the refresher, he can't have it in time, and the conclusion is guaranteed. Whether a refresher covers everything doesn't matter, since the rule asks only whether he completed training. What other teachers do, his record, and the rules for teachers on leave don't say when he'll have it.`, task:`LR05`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Greer: The city should keep the old fire station on Pearl Street and turn it into a museum. It was built in 1883, it's the oldest building downtown, and once a building like that is gone, it's gone for good.<br>Paulo: Being old isn't by itself a reason to keep a building standing. After the remodel in the 1970s, nothing of the original station is left but the outside walls.<br><br>Greer and Paulo disagree over whether`,
      opts:[`the fire station is the oldest building in the downtown area`,`the 1970s remodel changed the inside of the fire station`,`a museum would bring more visitors to the downtown area`,`the station's age is reason enough to keep it standing`,`the city can afford to keep the old station open to the public`],
      a:3, why:`Point at issue. Greer would keep it because it's the oldest building. Paulo says being old isn't by itself a reason. That's the disagreement. Paulo never disputes that it's the oldest, Greer never addresses the remodel, and neither of them mentions visitors or cost.`, task:`LR19`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`To win the county fair's pie prize, a pie must be made with fruit grown in the county. Mae's peach pie is made entirely with peaches from the family orchard, which sits just inside the county line. So Mae's pie will win this year's prize.<br><br>The reasoning in the argument is flawed because it`,
      opts:[`assumes that peaches are the only fruit grown anywhere in the county`,`treats a requirement for winning as if it guaranteed a win`,`relies on the judgment of someone who wants Mae's pie to win`,`overlooks the possibility that Mae's orchard is outside the county`,`takes for granted that a peach pie has won the prize in the past`],
      a:1, why:`Flaw. County fruit is necessary to win, not enough to win. Plenty of pies can meet the rule, and only one wins. The argument never assumes peaches are the only fruit, cites no one's judgment, says the orchard is inside the county, and doesn't rely on past winners.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Council member Ostby argues that the Tallis Street bridge needs major repairs before winter. But Ostby's brother owns the largest construction company in the county, which would likely bid on the repair work. So the bridge doesn't need major repairs.<br><br>Which one of the following most accurately describes a flaw in the argument?`,
      opts:[`It assumes that every construction company in the county would bid on the work.`,`It fails to say how much the repairs to the bridge would be likely to cost.`,`It rejects a claim over who stands to gain, without looking at the evidence for it.`,`It takes for granted that bridges need fewer repairs during the winter months.`,`It relies on the council member's own account of the condition of the bridge.`],
      a:2, why:`Flaw. A conflict of interest is a reason to check Ostby's evidence, not proof that the claim is false. The argument jumps from who might profit to the bridge being fine. Other bidders, cost and winter don't come into it, and the argument doesn't rely on Ostby's account. It dismisses it.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Town administrator: Last spring we passed a rule that sets quiet hours after 10 PM. Since the rule took effect, noise complaints made to the police after 10 PM have fallen by half. The rule has clearly made the town quieter at night.<br><br>Which one of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the administrator's argument?`,
      opts:[`Most of the noise complaints before the rule were about parties on weekends.`,`Neighboring towns with no quiet hours get about as many complaints as before.`,`The rule allows exceptions for a few holidays over the course of the year.`,`Noise complaints made before 10 PM have stayed about the same since spring.`,`Since spring, the police have required anyone who complains to give a name.`],
      a:4, why:`Weaken. If callers now have to give their names, many may stop calling, and complaints can fall with no change in the noise. Weekend parties, other towns and holiday exceptions don't undercut the link, and steady complaints before 10 PM, if anything, make the drop after 10 look more like the rule's doing.`, task:`LR08`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`,`STAT`]},
    {q:`On three small islands off the coast, a species of ground finch has noticeably shorter wings than the same species on the mainland. Records show that each island population was founded within the last four hundred years by birds blown over from the mainland, and none of the islands has any predator that hunts the finches.<br><br>Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?`,
      opts:[`A population of this finch can come to differ in wing length within a few centuries.`,`Finches with shorter wings are less able to escape predators than other finches.`,`The mainland finches will develop shorter wings if their predators disappear.`,`Every bird species that lives on an island without predators has shorter wings.`,`The finches on the islands fly less often than the finches on the mainland do.`],
      a:0, why:`Most strongly supported. The island birds came from the mainland within four hundred years and now have shorter wings, so wing length in this species can come to differ from its source within a few centuries. The passage never links wings to escape, predicts nothing about the mainland, covers one species only, and says nothing about how often the birds fly.`, task:`LR10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following principles is most clearly at work in the letter?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`A reviewer should search a first report for errors before reaching any opinion.`,`A second opinion is worth more when it reaches a different result from the first.`,`An expert should state the limits of what the evidence shows along with the findings.`,`The cause of a death should be judged by which of the injuries bled the most.`,`Hospital records should count for more than photographs in reviewing a death.`],
      a:2, why:`Principle. Solberg agrees on the cause of death, declines to order the injuries, and says she has kept her opinion to what the record shows. Stating limits along with findings is the rule she works by. She doesn't hunt for errors first, doesn't prize disagreement, judges the injuries by what they did and not by how they bled, and never ranks her sources.`, task:`RC12`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following can be inferred from the letter?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`The photographs taken at autopsy contradicted the original report.`,`The laceration and the fracture were caused by the same object.`,`Mr. Hensley lost consciousness at the moment of the first injury.`,`Nothing in the materials she reviewed fixes the order of the injuries.`,`Mr. Hensley's fall against the curb was caused by the blow to his brow.`],
      a:3, why:`Inference. She read the report, the photographs and the hospital record and says she can't tell from the record which injury came first, so none of those materials fixes the order. She agrees with the report instead of contradicting it, and nothing links the two injuries to one object, says when he lost consciousness, or ties the fall to the brow.`, task:`RC06`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following is most clearly an example of what the letter calls a non-contributory injury?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`A broken rib that punctured a lung and hastened a patient's death.`,`A bruised knee found on a man who died of a heart attack in his sleep.`,`A burn that became infected and led to the illness that killed a woman.`,`A blow to the chest that stopped a man's heart during a fight at a bar.`,`A cut that bled so much that the patient went into shock and then died.`],
      a:1, why:`Application. In the letter, non-contributory means a real injury that played no part in the death. A bruised knee on a man who died of a heart attack fits. The broken rib, the infected burn, the blow to the chest and the bleeding cut each helped bring about a death.`, task:`RC11`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The author's attitude toward the Berkeley researchers' study is best described as`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`skeptical, as an analysis that explained away real discrimination`,`indifferent, treating it as an episode of mostly historical interest`,`dismissive, as arithmetic that missed the larger social question`,`uncertain, as a study whose methods are still in serious dispute`,`approving, and mindful of the limits the authors set on it`],
      a:4, why:`Author's attitude. The author calls the study still taught as a warning, explains its method without a word of doubt, and ends by stressing a point the authors made themselves, that their count couldn't say why applicants lined up as they did. The author never doubts the analysis, treats it as alive, not historical, credits the authors with raising the social question, and reports no dispute over the methods.`, task:`RC03`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`According to the passage, the departments that drew many women applicants tended to`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`turn away most of the people who applied to them, men and women alike`,`admit women at much lower rates than they admitted men who applied`,`require more preparation in mathematics than other departments did`,`receive fewer applications than the departments that drew mostly men`,`be the departments that had been accused of bias against women`],
      a:0, why:`Specific detail. The passage says the departments that drew many women tended to be crowded ones that turned most people away, men and women alike. It says most departments showed no bias, never mentions mathematics, calls these departments crowded, not small, and names no department as accused.`, task:`RC05`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`The author would be most likely to agree with which one of the following statements?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`The Berkeley figures proved that no bias against women existed anywhere in admissions.`,`A campus wide admission rate is always misleading and shouldn't be reported at all.`,`Showing that those who decide were fair doesn't explain why the applicants differed.`,`The six departments that favored women outweighed the four that favored the men.`,`Simpson's paradox was first discovered by studying the Berkeley admissions data.`],
      a:2, why:`Would agree. The last paragraph says a department by department count can clear a committee but can't tell you why the applicants lined up as they did. Proved and anywhere go too far, and always is too strong. The passage counts six departments where men came out behind and four where women did, but it never says the six outweighed the four. The reversal came from combining the departments in a way that took account of where people applied. And it says statisticians already had a name for the reversal.`, task:`RC04`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following can most reasonably be inferred from the passage about the applicants of 1973?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Most of the women who applied were turned down by departments that favored men.`,`Women were less likely than men to apply to departments that admitted a bigger share of applicants.`,`Men who applied to English were admitted at lower rates than the women who applied.`,`Fewer than half of the departments had any women among their applicants that year.`,`The number of women applying to Berkeley fell in the years right after the study came out.`],
      a:1, why:`Inference. The departments that drew mostly men tended to admit a big share of whoever applied, and women applied more often to the crowded ones that turned most people away. So women were less likely to be applying where most got in. Only four departments admitted fewer women than expected, and nothing says most women applied to those four, so nothing supports it. The passage gives no rates for English by sex. At most sixteen of about a hundred departments had no women applicants, so most had some. And it says nothing about later years.`, task:`RC06`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`The author mentions English and mechanical engineering primarily in order to`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`show that some departments were biased against women and others against men`,`name two of the four departments where fewer women were admitted`,`suggest that engineering admitted a smaller share of applicants than English`,`illustrate that men and women didn't apply to the same departments`,`explain why sixteen departments were left out of the researchers' count`],
      a:3, why:`Function of a detail. The two departments come right after "Men and women hadn't applied to the same departments," as the example. Neither is called biased in either direction, and the passage never names the four. The crowded departments that drew women admitted the smaller share, the reverse of engineering admitting less. And the sixteen were left out for having no women applicants or turning nobody away, not because of these two.`, task:`RC08`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `He didn't say it again on the next Wednesday call, or the one after that, but he didn't take it back either. He talked about the heat. Rudy had a hearing date on his new expert. Somebody on the tier had gotten a picture of a puppy from home and passed it around until it came apart at the fold. The board sat on her kitchen table with her move still in it.`,
    `Ana had nothing from the lab. When they get to it, she said, every time Dani asked, which was every time.`,
    `Wednesday, August 18, at ten after four, her phone buzzed on her desk at the office with Ana's name on it, and she took it out to the stairwell.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Your brother was here at noon,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"On his lunch, with the grease still on his hands. He sat down and said he'll testify."</span>`,
    `Dani sat down on the step. Two floors down somebody let the street door bang shut.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I told him his father said no. He said his father doesn't get a vote on what he says to a judge."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then he asked me to call you. He said if he told you himself, you'd start helping him with it, and after that he'd never know which part was his."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"What did you tell him?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That I'd call you."</span> Ana's chair creaked. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Your father's my client. Your brother isn't. I'll work out what that means, and I'm not going to work it out on the phone with you sitting in a stairwell. Go back to your desk."</span>`,
    `She went back to her desk. She did the rest of the afternoon's work and some of tomorrow's, and took the 5:40 bus home with the prep book shut on her lap. There wasn't any test in it anymore. She kept carrying it for the envelope.`,
    `At ten to seven she stood at the kitchen table over the board. It had been her move since May. She'd looked at it every night for three months and never once picked anything up.`,
    `She moved her knight up to the middle of the board. He'd have to answer it or leave it there, and either way it was his turn.`,
    `At seven she was on the step with the phone, and it rang. Recording, then him, and she gave him the move before he could ask for it, knight to the middle, the square by number like he'd taught her at eight on the back of a cereal box.`,
    `Then she waited while he found it on his own board, and listened to the tier behind him, a TV, somebody at the next phone arguing about money, a door. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"There she is,"</span> he said.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch10.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Hearing`
});
